window.docContents[233].resolve({"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Proposition-6___6___7___-Reflection-of-indecomposable-is-indecomposable-or-0/#Chapter6___Proposition6___6___7":{"contents":"Proposition 6.6.7. Let Q be a quiver, and let V be an indecomposable representation of Q. Then F_i^+ V and F_i^- V (whenever defined) are either indecomposable or 0.\n\nProof. We prove the proposition for F_i^+ V; the case F_i^- V follows similarly. By Proposition 6.6.5 it follows that either\n\n\n\\varphi : \\bigoplus_{j \\to i} V_j \\to V_i\n\n\nis surjective or \\dim V_i = 1, \\dim V_j = 0, j \\neq i. In the last case\n\n\nF_i^+ V = 0.\n\n\nSo we can assume that \\varphi is surjective. In this case, assume that F_i^+ V is decomposable as\n\n\nF_i^+ V = X \\oplus Y\n\nwith X, Y \\neq 0. But F_i^+ V is injective at i, since the maps are canonical projections, whose direct sum is the tautological embedding. Therefore X and Y also have to be injective at i and hence (by Proposition 6.6.6)\n\n\nF_i^+ F_i^- X = X, \\quad F_i^+ F_i^- Y = Y.\n\n\nIn particular\n\n\nF_i^- X \\neq 0, \\quad F_i^- Y \\neq 0.\n\n\nTherefore\n\n\nV = F_i^- F_i^+ V = F_i^- X \\oplus F_i^- Y,\n\n\nwhich is a contradiction, since V was assumed to be indecomposable. So we can infer that\n\n\nF_i^+ V\n\n\nis indecomposable. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.6. Reflection functors\tProposition 6.6.7: Reflection of indecomposable is indecomposable or 0","header":"Proposition 6.6.7: Reflection of indecomposable is indecomposable or 0","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Proposition-6___6___7___-Reflection-of-indecomposable-is-indecomposable-or-0/#Chapter6___Proposition6___6___7"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/Weyl-character-formula___-vanishing-criterion___-character-of-L_lambda___-and-dimension-formula/#Chapter5___Theorem5___22___1":{"contents":"Theorem 5.22.1 (Weyl character formula). The representation L_\\lambda is zero if and only if N < p, where p is the number of parts of \\lambda. If N \\geq p, the character of L_\\lambda is the Schur polynomial S_\\lambda(x). Therefore, the dimension of L_\\lambda is given by the formula\n\n\\dim L_\\lambda = \\prod_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq N} \\frac{\\lambda_i - \\lambda_j + j - i}{j - i}.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.22. The characters of $L_\\lambda$\tWeyl character formula: vanishing criterion, character of L_lambda, and dimension formula","header":"Weyl character formula: vanishing criterion, character of L_lambda, and dimension formula","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/Weyl-character-formula___-vanishing-criterion___-character-of-L_lambda___-and-dimension-formula/#Chapter5___Theorem5___22___1"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Problem-6___1___5___-Finite-type-quivers/#Chapter6___Problem6___1___5":{"contents":"Problem 6.1.5. Let Q be a quiver with a set of vertices D. We say that Q is of finite type if it has finitely many indecomposable representations. Let b_{ij} be the number of directed edges from i to j in Q (i, j \\in D).\n\nWe have the following remarkable theorem, proved by P. Gabriel in the early 1970s.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.1. Problems\tProblem 6.1.5: Finite type quivers","header":"Problem 6.1.5: Finite type quivers","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Problem-6___1___5___-Finite-type-quivers/#Chapter6___Problem6___1___5"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Forgetful-functor-as-representable-functor/#Chapter7___Example7___5___3":{"contents":"Example 7.5.3. Let A be an algebra. Then the forgetful functor from the category of left A-modules to the category of vector spaces is representable, and the representing object is the free rank 1 module (= the regular representation) M = A. But if A is infinite dimensional and we restrict attention to the category of finite dimensional modules, then the forgetful functor, in general, is not representable (this is so, for example, if A is the algebra of complex functions on \\mathbb{Z} which are zero at all but finitely many points).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.5. Representable functors\tForgetful functor as representable functor","header":"Forgetful functor as representable functor","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Forgetful-functor-as-representable-functor/#Chapter7___Example7___5___3"}});
